## bluez-async 0.8.2

The pinned source is bundled in rust/vendor/bluez-async. Its original file hashes
and the local constructor extension are recorded in bluez-async-source.json and
bluez-async.patch. new_resource exposes the unspawned D-Bus I/O future so btleplug
can own, cancel and join the actual transport task. Message-stream Drop now
retires local callbacks synchronously and queues remote match removal inside that
transport future, with at most 64 queued and 16 running removals. Removal/overflow
errors are retained for explicit shutdown. No separate match-removal task is
spawned. The existing new constructor keeps its signature and also uses this
owned cleanup path. License: MIT OR Apache-2.0; exact-commit upstream license texts are
in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES and rust/licenses/sources.json.

The BlueZ service/characteristic/descriptor discovery paths validate required
relative child-node names before object-path construction and return existing
metadata error types instead of panicking. The standalone upstream test lock
uses serde-xml-rs 0.8.2, matching the production parser and replacing its inherited
yanked xml-rs dependency. OpenBLE's production dependency graph is unchanged.

Local BlueZ enumeration preserves malformed adapter/device metadata errors while
ignoring unrelated D-Bus interfaces. Adapter-state query errors propagate instead
of being reported as powered off.

The owned Linux D-Bus task now publishes sticky transport completion to adapter
event streams. Each stream delivers one terminal error and retires its event
registration before delivery, without a separate spawned monitor task.

D-Bus signal delivery now uses a bounded 256-message queue per match. Overflow
retires publication and records one background failure, terminating the shared
owned transport through its existing error path. Consumer closure does not
report overflow; local/remote match removal remains explicit during stream Drop.

Linux device lookup maps only D-Bus UnknownObject to DeviceNotFound. Other bus,
permission and metadata failures retain their native error cause.

BlueZ opaque-ID helpers preserve root/shallow parent paths and format validated
paths outside the usual prefix without panicking. Existing BlueZ ID formatting
and public method signatures remain unchanged.

dbus 0.9.12 is bundled from its checksum-verified crates.io source under its
upstream Apache-2.0/MIT licensing. Published file hashes and reviewed changes
are recorded in rust/vendor/dbus-source.json and rust/vendor/dbus.patch.
The local SyncConnection method-reply guard retires canceled/timed-out reply
callbacks without retaining the native connection. Registration identity protects
reused serials; callbacks run outside the reply registry lock. Send wakeup failure
also retires its callback. Other connection types retain their prior method-reply
cancellation behavior. No D-Bus C implementation is modified or bundled by this
change; Linux still links the system client library.

Linux result-bearing notification streams now observe the shared transport task
directly and publish its terminal cause before ending. The legacy value-only
stream ends on that failure. Registration ownership retires before terminal
delivery; no additional notification monitor task is spawned.

Linux asynchronous adapter enumeration and event/notification registration now
observe that same owned transport completion. Failure retires pending setup and
partial event ownership; cancellation releases the observer without spawning an
additional task. This does not change synchronous initial C bus authentication.


dbus-tokio 0.7.6 is bundled from its checksum-verified crates.io archive under
Apache-2.0/MIT. Original file hashes and the local reactor readiness patch are in
rust/vendor/dbus-tokio-source.json and rust/vendor/dbus-tokio.patch. Authentication
can change a readable watch into a writable watch without first attempting output;
the patch retains existing readiness for that transition and observes native write
interest before treating queued messages as blocked writes. Both readiness interests
are registered. The Linux manager uses awaitable, inline authentication/Hello with
no bootstrap task. Channel unique-name installation requires exclusive ownership
and validates/copies the Hello name. The old synchronous Rust constructor remains
available. No libdbus C source is modified; socket opening is still synchronous.

The local dbus-tokio reactor also separates its waker state mutex from caller
clone/wake/drop hooks and Tokio readiness polling. State commits preserve queued
send readiness across re-entry; disposal of the old registration occurs after
unlocking. The private wake module and focused tests are included in the reviewed
patch inventory. Public Rust and Dart APIs remain unchanged.

Windows value read/write and notification setup/teardown consistently apply the
existing GATT status classification. Failed reads do not access Value and failed
unsubscribe retains its handler ownership for retry. Protocol/unknown result
statuses now carry an owned raw WinRT status for native diagnostics; this is not
a detailed ATT error byte. PermissionDenied/NotConnected variants and WinRT
HRESULT handling remain intact. The local private mapper is original OpenBLE code.

Windows retrieval service queries are deferred until service matching is needed.
Non-stale query failures remain explicit; the existing Unreachable/timeout omission
policy remains. A private selector helper and focused tests are original OpenBLE
code. This change does not alter identifier-only retrieval or claim native WinRT
cancellation validation.

Windows identifier retrieval preserves lookup failures and accepts known cached
devices that are disconnected. Null-result omission requires confirmed successful
completion; the private classifier and its regressions are original OpenBLE code.
Public APIs and service/combined enumeration semantics remain unchanged.

Apple disconnect delegate callbacks copy native error diagnostics for pending
operation replies. Confirmed disconnect cleanup still succeeds; notification
compatibility does not suppress disconnect errors. Public event/API shapes remain
unchanged. The focused Objective-C fixture regression is original OpenBLE code.

Apple disconnect cleanup retires internal ownership before public event delivery.
An unused internal disconnect event and its ignored reader arm were removed;
power-off retirement completes for all peripherals before event publication.
The private retirement helper and focused backpressure tests are OpenBLE additions.
Public Rust/Dart APIs and the C ABI remain unchanged.

Windows pending notification setup owns a private synchronous cleanup guard.
Cancellation/failure retires the existing handler and attempts removal; failed
removal retains its token for retry. Successful setup commits ownership. The
guard and portable regression cases are original OpenBLE additions. Public APIs
are unchanged; native CCCD cancellation/COM release is not claimed by these tests.
